<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ryukyu Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1872 CE – 1879 CE · capital Shuri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The transitional status imposed on the Ryukyu Kingdom by Meiji Japan from 1872 until its annexation as Okinawa Prefecture in 1879.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the abolition of domains in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1871, the Meiji government moved to assert sole control over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu</a>, which had long been a tributary of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a>. In 1872 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-tai">King Sho Tai</a> was named lord of the new Ryukyu Domain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The royal government at Shuri continued to run local affairs, but foreign relations passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Tokyo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> used the killing of shipwrecked Ryukyuan sailors in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Taiwan</a> in 1871 to launch its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> expedition of 1874, presenting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Ryukyuans</a> as Japanese subjects. In 1875 Tokyo ordered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu</a> to stop sending tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which the court resisted.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1879 the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsuda-michiyuki">Matsuda Michiyuki</a> arrived with police and troops, abolished the domain and created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-prefecture">Okinawa Prefecture</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-tai">Sho Tai</a> was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Tokyo</a>. The Qing protested, but the annexation stood.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ryukyu-domain.jpg" alt="Ryukyu Domain" width="320" height="322"><figcaption>Image: BraneJ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hidari_mitsudomoe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1872 CE – 1879 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shuri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sho Tai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ryukyuan religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Okinawan, Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ryukyu han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=ryukyu-domain" aria-label="Ryukyu Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8052 1215 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ryukyu Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8372 1471L8372 1471M8372 1471L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8372 1471h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8372 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8372 1471h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shuri. <a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=ryukyu-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
